e + w \\)\n- \\( |G \\cap S| = c + f + w \\)\n- \\( |D \\cap G \\cap S| = w \\)\n\nBut without knowing \\( d, e, f \\), it's difficult.\n\nAlternatively, we can use the inclusion-exclusion formula and solve for \\( w \\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 195 + 367 + 562 - (d + e + f) + w = 1124 - (d + e + f) + w \\]\nBut \\( |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 900 - x \\), and \\( x = 229 - w \\), so:\n\\[ 900 - (229 - w) = 1124 - (d + e + f) + w \\]\n\\[ 671 + w = 1124 - (d + e + f) + w \\]\n\\[ 671 = 1124 - (d + e + f) \\]\n\\[ d + e + f = 1124 - 671 = 453 \\]\n\nBut we know that \\( d + e + f \\) is the number of residents owning exactly two items but not all four, which is 437. Wait, no. \\( d + e + f \\) is the number of residents owning exactly two items, which is 437, but \\( d + e + f = 437 \\). Therefore:\n\\[ 437 = 453 \\]\nThis is a contradiction, which means our approach is wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps \\( d + e + f = 437 \\). But from the inclusion-exclusion principle:\n\\[ |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 1124 - 437 + w = 1124 - 437 + w = 687 + w \\]\nBut \\( |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 900 - x \\), and \\( x = 229 - w \\), so:\n\\[ 900 - (229 - w) = 687 + w \\]\n\\[ 671 + w = 687 + w \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ 671 = 687 \\]\nWhich is impossible. Therefore, our initial assumption must be wrong.\n\nPerhaps we need to consider that \\( |D \\cap G| \\), \\( |D \\cap S| \\), \\( |G \\cap S| \\) include the residents who own all four items. Therefore, we should subtract \\( w \\) from each intersection.\n\nSo, correct expressions:\n\\[ |D \\cap G| = a + d + w \\]\n\\[ |D \\cap S| = a + e + w \\]\n\\[ |G \\cap S| = b + f + w \\]\n\\[ |D \\cap G \\cap S| = w \\]\n\nBut we don't know \\( a, d, e, f, b, f \\). However, we know:\n- \\( a + b + w = 900 - x \\)\n- \\( a + b + w = 671 + w \\)\n- \\( x = 229 - w \\)\n\nThis isn't helping. Maybe another approach.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n- \\( x \\) = only candy hearts.\n- \\( y \\) = exactly two items.\n- \\( z \\) = exactly three items.\n- \\( w