\\cap S| = c + w \\) (c is exactly two, D and S)\n- \\( |G \\cap S| = b + w \\) (b is exactly two, G and S)\n- \\( |D \\cap G \\cap S| = w \\)\n\nBut without knowing \\( a, b, c \\), it's difficult. However, we can express the total.\n\nFrom the inclusion-exclusion principle:\n\\[ |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 195 + 367 + 562 - |D \\cap G| - |D \\cap S| - |G \\cap S| + w \\]\n\nBut we also know that:\n\\[ |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 900 - x = 900 - (229 - w) = 671 + w \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[ 671 + w = 195 + 367 + 562 - |D \\cap G| - |D \\cap S| - |G \\cap S| + w \\]\n\\[ 671 + w = 1124 - (|D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S|) + w \\]\nSubtract \\( w \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 671 = 1124 - (|D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S|) \\]\n\\[ |D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S| = 1124 - 671 = 453 \\]\n\nBut \\( |D \\cap G| = a + w \\), \\( |D \\cap S| = c + w \\), \\( |G \\cap S| = b + w \\). So:\n\\[ (a + w) + (c + w) + (b + w) = 453 \\]\n\\[ (a + b + c) + 3w = 453 \\]\nBut \\( a + b + c = 437 \\) (from the exactly two items). Therefore:\n\\[ 437 + 3w = 453 \\]\n\\[ 3w = 453 - 437 = 16 \\]\n\\[ w = 16 / 3 \\approx 5.333 \\]\n\nWait, that can't be right because the number of residents must be an integer. So, I must have made a mistake.\n\nLet me re-examine the steps.\n\nWe had:\n\\[ |D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S| = 453 \\]\nBut \\( |D \\cap G| = a + w \\), \\( |D \\cap S| = c + w \\), \\( |G \\cap S| = b + w \\)\nSo:\n\\[ (a + w) + (c + w) + (b + w) = 453 \\]\n\\[ (a + b + c) + 3w = 453 \\]\nBut \\( a + b + c = 437 \\)\nSo:\n\\[ 437 + 3w = 453 \\]\n\\[ 3w = 16 \\]\n\\[ w = 16/3 \\approx 5.333 \\]\n\nHmm, still not an integer. That suggests an error in my reasoning.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in calculating \\( |D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S| \\).\n\nWait, when we did inclusion-exclusion, we had:\n\n\\[ |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 671 + w = 1124 - (|D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S|) \\]\nSo:\n\\[ |D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S| = 1124 - (671 + w) = 453 - w \\]\n\nWait, no, that's not correct. Let me re-express:\n\nFrom the inclusion-exclusion:\n\n\\[ |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 1124 - (|D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S|) + |D \\cap G \\cap S| \\]\nBut \\( |D \\cap G \\cap S| = w \\), so:\n\\[ |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 1124 - (|D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S|) + w \\]\nBut we also know \\( |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 671 + w \\), so:\n\\[ 671 + w = 1124 - (|D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S|) + w \\]\nSubtract \\( w \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 671 = 1124 - (|D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S|) \\]\n\\[ |D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S| = 1124 - 671 = 453 \\]\n\nSo that part was correct.\n\nThen, \\( |D \\cap G| = a + w \\), \\( |D \\cap S| = c + w \\), \\( |G \\cap S| = b + w \\). So:\n\n\\[ (a + w) + (c + w) + (b + w) = 453 \\]\n\\[ (a + b + c) + 3w = 453 \\]\nBut \\( a + b + c = 437 \\), so:\n\\[ 437 + 3w = 453 \\]\n\\[ 3w = 16 \\]\n\\[ w = 16/3 \\]\n\nStill, this gives a fractional value. That suggests that perhaps my initial assumption is wrong or there is a miscalculation.\n\nWait, perhaps I miscounted the number of residents owning exactly two or three items.\n\nWait, the problem says 437 own exactly two, 234 own exactly three. So total is 437 + 234 = 671, plus those owning exactly one or all four.\n\nBut total residents are 900, so those owning exactly one or all four are 900 - 671 = 229. So, x + w = 229, where x is those owning exactly one, and w is those owning all four.\n\nSo, x = 229 - w.\n\nBut how does that help?\n\nWait, let's go back.\n\nFrom inclusion-exclusion, we have:\n\n\\[ |D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S| = 453 \\]\nBut \\( |D \\cap G| = a + w \\), \\( |D \\cap S| = c + w \\), \\( |G \\cap S| = b + w \\)\nSo:\n\\[ (a + w) + (c + w) + (b + w) = 453 \\]\n\\[ (a + b + c) + 3w = 453 \\]\nBut \\( a + b + c = 437 \\), so:\n\\[ 437 + 3w = 453 \\]\n\\[ 3w = 16 \\]\n\\[ w = \\frac{16}{3} \\approx 5.333 \\]\n\nThis is not possible because the number of residents must be an integer. Therefore, there must be a mistake in the reasoning.\n\nWait, perhaps the assumption that \\( |D \\cap G| = a + w \\) is incorrect.\n\nWait, actually, \\( |D \\cap G| \\) includes those who own both D and G, which includes those who own exactly D and G (a) and those who own D, G, and possibly more (w). So, yes, \\( |D \\cap G| = a + w \\).\n\nSimilarly, \\( |D \\cap S| = c + w \\), \\( |G \\cap S| = b + w \\).\n\nThus, the equation \\( 437 + 3w = 453 \\) seems correct, leading to \\( w = 16/3 \\). But that can't be, so perhaps the initial counts are wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps I miscalculated the inclusion-exclusion.\n\nWait, let's recast the inclusion-exclusion formula:\n\n\\[ |D \\cup G \\cup S| = |D| + |G| + |S| - |D \\cap G| - |D \\cap S| - |G \\cap S| + |D \\cap G \\cap S| \\]\n\nWe have:\n\n\\[ |D \\cup G \\cup S| = 671 + w \\]\n\\[ |D| + |G| + |S| = 195 + 367 + 562 = 1124 \\]\n\\[ |D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S| = 453 \\]\n\\[ |D \\cap G \\cap S| = w \\]\n\nSo substituting:\n\n\\[ 671 + w = 1124 - 453 + w \\]\n\\[ 671 + w = 671 + w \\]\n\nWhich is an identity, giving us no new information.\n\nTherefore, the equation \\( 437 + 3w = 453 \\) comes from the equation \\( |D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S| = 453 \\) and \\( |D \\cap G| = a + w \\), etc., so that gives \\( 437 + 3w = 453 \\), which leads to \\( w = 16/3 \\). But this is a contradiction because \\( w \\) must be an integer.\n\nThis suggests that perhaps the problem has overlapping sets where some residents own all four, but the counts don't add up because of the way the intersections are counted.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that some residents are being double-counted or something else.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mistake is that when we count \\( |D \\cap G| \\), it's equal to \\( a + w \\), but actually, \\( |D \\cap G| \\) is the number of residents who own both D and G, which includes those who own exactly two (a) and those who own all four (w). So, that part is correct.\n\nSimilarly for the others.\n\nSo, the equation seems correct, but it leads to a fractional value, which is impossible. Therefore, maybe the problem has a typo, or perhaps my approach is wrong.\n\nWait, another approach: Let me denote:\n\n- Let x be the number of residents who own exactly one item.\n\n- y be the number who own exactly two.\n\n- z be the number who own exactly three.\n\n- w be the number who own all four.\n\nGiven:\n\n- x + y + z + w = 900\n\n- y = 437\n\n- z = 234\n\n- x + y + z + w = 900 => x + 437 + 234 + w = 900 => x + w = 229 => x = 229 - w\n\nAlso, from inclusion-exclusion:\n\nThe number of residents owning at least one item is:\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = |D| + |G| + |S| - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S|\n\nBut |D \u222a G \u222a S| = total residents - those who own nothing. But everyone owns a bag of candy hearts, so everyone is in at least D, G, or S? Wait, no, they all own a bag of candy hearts, but perhaps not D, G, or S. Wait, no, the problem says each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts, but they may or may not own D, G, or S.\n\nWait, actually, the problem says:\n\n\"Among the 900 residents of Aimeville, there are 195 who own a diamond ring, 367 who own a set of golf clubs, and 562 who own a garden spade. In addition, each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts. There are 437 residents who own exactly two of these things, and 234 residents who own exactly three of these things.\"\n\nSo, each resident owns a bag of candy hearts, but may or may not own D, G, or S.\n\nSo, the total number of residents is 900, each owning a bag of candy hearts, but some may not own D, G, or S.\n\nTherefore, |D \u222a G \u222a S| is the number of residents who own at least one of D, G, or S. Thus, |D \u222a G \u222a S| = 900 - x, where x is the number of residents who own only candy hearts.\n\nBut from the problem, the number of residents who own exactly two is 437, exactly three is 234, and exactly one is x, and all four is w. So, total is 437 + 234 + x + w = 671 + x + w = 900. Therefore, x + w = 229.\n\nBut |D \u222a G \u222a S| is the number of residents who own at least one of D, G, or S, which is equal to those who own exactly one (but in addition to candy hearts) plus those who own two, three, or four.\n\nWait, no. Actually, |D \u222a G \u222a S| is the number of residents who own D, or G, or S. Since everyone owns candy hearts, but candy hearts are separate from D, G, S.\n\nWait, actually, candy hearts are a separate item. So, the residents can own candy hearts plus nothing else, or candy hearts plus one, two, three, or all four of D, G, S.\n\nTherefore, |D \u222a G \u222a S| is the number of residents who own D, or G, or S. So, that is equal to the number of residents who own D, G, or S, regardless of whether they own candy hearts or not.\n\nBut since candy hearts are a separate item, and all residents own candy hearts, |D \u222a G \u222a S| is the number of residents who own at least one of D, G, or S.\n\nSo, to compute |D \u222a G \u222a S|, we can use inclusion-exclusion:\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = |D| + |G| + |S| - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S|\n\nWhich is 195 + 367 + 562 - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S|\n\nBut |D \u2229 G| is the number of residents who own both D and G, which includes those who own exactly two (a) and those who own all four (w). Similarly, |D \u2229 S| = c + w, |G \u2229 S| = b + w.\n\nAnd |D \u2229 G \u2229 S| = w.\n\nSo, substituting:\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = 195 + 367 + 562 - (a + w) - (c + w) - (b + w) + w\n\nSimplify:\n\n= 1124 - a - w - c - w - b - w + w\n\n= 1124 - (a + b + c) - 3w + w\n\n= 1124 - (a + b + c) - 2w\n\nBut a + b + c = 437 (since these are the residents owning exactly two items), so:\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = 1124 - 437 - 2w = 687 - 2w\n\nBut |D \u222a G \u222a S| is also equal to the number of residents who own at least one of D, G, or S, which is equal to the total residents minus those who own only candy hearts.\n\nTotal residents = 900\n\nResidents who own only candy hearts = x\n\nTherefore, |D \u222a G \u222a S| = 900 - x\n\nSo,\n\n900 - x = 687 - 2w\n\nBut we know that x + w = 229, so x = 229 - w\n\nSubstitute:\n\n900 - (229 - w) = 687 - 2w\n\nSimplify:\n\n900 - 229 + w = 687 - 2w\n\n671 + w = 687 - 2w\n\nBring variables to one side:\n\nw + 2w = 687 - 671\n\n3w = 16\n\nw = 16/3 \u2248 5.333\n\nAgain, we get w = 16/3, which is not an integer.\n\nThis suggests that either the problem has no solution, which is impossible, or I made a miscalculation.\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the inclusion-exclusion formula.\n\nWait, let me recast:\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = |D| + |G| + |S| - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S|\n\nWe have |D| = 195, |G| = 367, |S| = 562\n\n|D \u2229 G| = a + w, |D \u2229 S| = c + w, |G \u2229 S| = b + w\n\n|D \u2229 G \u2229 S| = w\n\nTherefore,\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = 195 + 367 + 562 - (a + w) - (c + w) - (b + w) + w\n\nSimplify:\n\n= 1124 - (a + b + c) - 3w + w\n\n= 1124 - (a + b + c) - 2w\n\nBut a + b + c = 437, so:\n\n= 1124 - 437 - 2w = 687 - 2w\n\nBut |D \u222a G \u222a S| = 900 - x, and x = 229 - w\n\nSo:\n\n900 - (229 - w) = 687 - 2w\n\nSimplify:\n\n900 - 229 + w = 687 - 2w\n\n671 + w = 687 - 2w\n\nSo:\n\n3w = 687 - 671 = 16\n\nw = 16/3\n\nSame result.\n\nHmm, so unless I have a miscalculation, perhaps the problem is designed this way, and the answer is 16/3, but that can't be, since number of residents must be integer.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is that some residents own only candy hearts, and that's it, so they don't own D, G, or S.\n\nThus, when we compute |D \u222a G \u222a S|, it's 900 - x, as above.\n\nBut if |D \u222a G \u222a S| = 687 - 2w, and also = 900 - x, and x = 229 - w, then:\n\n687 - 2w = 900 - (229 - w)\n\n687 - 2w = 671 + w\n\nThen,\n\n687 - 671 = 3w\n\n16 = 3w\n\nw = 16/3\n\nSame result.\n\nSo, perhaps, despite the fractional answer, the correct answer is 16/3, but that doesn't make sense.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that the residents who own exactly two items include those who own two of the four things, but not necessarily D, G, S.\n\nWait, the problem says \"residents who own exactly two of these things\", where \"these things\" refers to diamond ring, golf clubs, garden spade, and bag of candy hearts.\n\nWait, that's a key point. So, if someone owns two of these four things, they could be owning two among D, G, S, and candy hearts.\n\nBut in the initial analysis, I considered that exactly two refers to D, G, S, but not candy hearts.\n\nBut the problem says \"exactly two of these things\", so it includes the possibility of owning candy hearts and one other.\n\nSimilarly, exactly three refers to three of these four things.\n\nSo, that changes the calculation.\n\nTherefore, my previous approach was wrong because I considered only D, G, S, but in reality, \"these things\" include candy hearts as well.\n\nSo, we need to adjust the counts accordingly.\n\nLet me redefine:\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\n- Let x be the number of residents who own exactly one of D, G, S, and candy hearts.\n\n- Let y be the number of residents who own exactly two (including candy hearts).\n\n- Let z be the number of residents who own exactly three (including candy hearts).\n\n- Let w be the number of residents who own all four.\n\nGiven:\n\n- Total residents: 900\n\n- Residents owning exactly two: 437 (so y = 437)\n\n- Residents owning exactly three: 234 (so z = 234)\n\n- Residents owning exactly one: x\n\n- Residents owning all four: w\n\nThus, x + y + z + w = 900 => x + 437 + 234 + w = 900 => x + w = 229 => x = 229 - w\n\nNow, compute |D \u222a G \u222a S|:\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = |D| + |G| + |S| - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S|\n\nBut now, |D| includes those who own D and possibly candy hearts, similarly for |G| and |S|.\n\nBut the problem is that the overlaps include candy hearts.\n\nSo, |D| = number of residents owning D (regardless of candy hearts). Similarly, |G| = residents owning G, |S| = residents owning S.\n\nSo, |D| = a + b + c + w, where a is exactly D, b is D and candy hearts, c is D and something else, but wait, no.\n\nWait, actually, |D| is the number of residents owning D, regardless of what else they own.\n\nSimilarly, |G| and |S|.\n\nSo, to compute |D \u222a G \u222a S|, we need to consider all residents who own D, G, or S, regardless of whether they own candy hearts or not.\n\nSo, the residents owning D, G, or S can be broken down as:\n\n- Those owning only D\n\n- Those owning D and G\n\n- Those owning D and S\n\n- Those owning G and S\n\n- Those owning D, G, and S\n\n- Those owning all four (D, G, S, candy hearts)\n\nBut also, some residents might own only candy hearts.\n\nSo, let me define:\n\nLet\u2019s use the following variables:\n\n- a: exactly D\n\n- b: exactly G\n\n- c: exactly S\n\n- d: exactly D and G\n\n- e: exactly D and S\n\n- f: exactly G and S\n\n- g: exactly D, G, and S\n\n- h: exactly all four (D, G, S, candy hearts)\n\nSo, total residents:\n\na + b + c + d + e + f + g + h + (residents owning only candy hearts) = 900\n\nBut residents owning only candy hearts: let's denote this as x.\n\nThus:\n\na + b + c + d + e + f + g + h + x = 900\n\nBut from the problem:\n\nTotal residents owning exactly two: d + e + f + (those owning exactly two including candy hearts) = 437\n\nWait, no. The problem says \"residents who own exactly two of these things\", where \"these things\" are D, G, S, and candy hearts.\n\nThus, residents owning exactly two can be:\n\n- d: D and G\n\n- e: D and S\n\n- f: G and S\n\n- k: D and candy hearts\n\n- l: G and candy hearts\n\n- m: S and candy hearts\n\nSimilarly, residents owning exactly three can be:\n\n- g: D, G, S\n\n- n: D, G, candy hearts\n\n- o: D, S, candy hearts\n\n- p: G, S, candy hearts\n\nAnd residents owning all four: h: D, G, S, candy hearts\n\nAnd residents owning exactly one: a, b, c.\n\nSo, this complicates the counts.\n\nBut given the problem statement, it's better to think in terms of the Venn diagram with four sets: D, G, S, CH (candy hearts).\n\nEach resident can be in any combination of these four sets.\n\nBut the counts given are:\n\n- Total residents: 900\n\n- Number owning exactly two: 437\n\n- Number owning exactly three: 234\n\n- Number owning all four: h\n\n- Number owning exactly one: a + b + c\n\n- Number owning exactly two: includes those owning two among D, G, S, CH or two including CH.\n\nSimilarly, exactly three includes those owning three among D, G, S, CH.\n\nSo, the problem is that the initial approach didn't account for the fact that \"exactly two\" can include candy hearts.\n\nThis complicates the inclusion-exclusion.\n\nTherefore, perhaps a better approach is to model this with the principle of inclusion-exclusion considering all four sets.\n\nBut that's complicated.\n\nAlternatively, let me consider the following:\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\n- Let T be the total number of residents: 900\n\n- Let A be the set of residents owning a diamond ring: |A| = 195\n\n- Let B be the set owning golf clubs: |B| = 367\n\n- Let C be the set owning a garden spade: |C| = 562\n\n- Let D be the set owning a bag of candy hearts: |D| = 900 (since everyone owns it)\n\nWe need to find the number of residents owning all four: |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = w\n\nWe know:\n\n- Number of residents owning exactly two of these four things: 437\n\n- Number owning exactly three: 234\n\n- Number owning exactly one: x = 229 - w\n\n- Number owning exactly four: w\n\nSo, total residents: x + 437 + 234 + w = 900 => x + w = 229\n\nWe need to compute w.\n\nWe can use inclusion-exclusion for four sets, but since everyone owns D, it's a bit different.\n\nWait, since everyone owns D, the counts for A, B, C are subsets of D.\n\nSo, |A \u222a B \u222a C| is the number of residents owning at least one of A, B, C, which is equal to |A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|\n\nBut since everyone owns D, the union |A \u222a B \u222a C| is the number of residents owning at least one of A, B, or C, which is equal to the number of residents owning candy hearts and at least one of A, B, or C.\n\nBut since everyone owns candy hearts, |A \u222a B \u222a C| = |A \u222a B \u222a C \u222a D| = |A \u222a B \u222a C|\n\nBut since D is everyone, |A \u222a B \u222a C| = |A \u222a B \u222a C| = total residents - those who own only D.\n\nBut those who own only D are x - w, since x is residents owning exactly one, which can be D or something else, but wait, no.\n\nWait, x is the number of residents owning exactly one, which can be either A, B, C, or D.\n\nBut since everyone owns D, the only way to own exactly one is to own only D.\n\nBecause if someone owns exactly one, they must own only D, because they already own candy hearts.\n\nWait, hold on.\n\nWait, no. The problem says \"residents who own exactly two of these things\", so \"these things\" are A, B, C, D.\n\nSo, if someone owns exactly one, it can be one of A, B, C, or D.\n\nBut since everyone owns D, the only way to own exactly one is to own only D.\n\nBecause if someone owns exactly one, they can't own D and something else, because that would be exactly two.\n\nWait, no. If someone owns exactly one, it's one of the four things. Since they own D anyway, the only way to own exactly one is to own only D.\n\nTherefore, the number of residents owning exactly one is the number of residents owning only D.\n\nThus, x = number of residents owning only D.\n\nSimilarly, residents owning exactly two can be:\n\n- Those owning exactly two among A, B, C, and D (including D and one other)\n\n- Or, since they already own D, owning exactly two would mean D and one other.\n\nWait, no. If someone owns exactly two, it's two of the four things, regardless of whether they own D or not.\n\nBut since everyone owns D, owning exactly two would mean D and one other.\n\nBut the problem says \"residents who own exactly two of these things\", meaning two of the four, so could be D and one of A, B, C.\n\nSimilarly, exactly three would be three of the four, which could be D and two others.\n\nAnd exactly four would be all four.\n\nTherefore, to model this:\n\nLet\u2019s define:\n\n- Let a: own exactly A only\n\n- b: own exactly B only\n\n- c: own exactly C only\n\n- d: own exactly D only\n\n- e: own D and A only\n\n- f: own D and B only\n\n- g: own D and C only\n\n- h: own D, A, and B only\n\n- i: own D, A, and C only\n\n- j: own D, B, and C only\n\n- k: own D, A, B, and C\n\nSo, total residents:\n\na + b + c + d + e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 900\n\nGiven:\n\n- a + b + c + d = x (exactly one)\n\n- e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 437 + 234 = 671 (exactly two or more)\n\nBut specifically:\n\n- Exactly two: e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 437\n\n- Exactly three: h + i + j + k = 234\n\n- Exactly four: k = w\n\nAlso, we have:\n\n|A| = a + e + h + i + k = 195\n\n|B| = b + f + h + i + k = 367\n\n|C| = c + g + h + i + k = 562\n\n|D| = d + e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 900 (since everyone owns D)\n\nSo, now we have a system of equations:\n\n1. a + b + c + d + e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 900\n\n2. a + b + c + d = x\n\n3. e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 671\n\n4. h + i + j + k = 234\n\n5. k = w\n\n6. a + e + h + i + k = 195\n\n7. b + f + h + i + k = 367\n\n8. c + g + h + i + k = 562\n\n9. d + e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 900\n\nSo, we can substitute:\n\nFrom equation 9: d + e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 900\n\nBut from equation 3: e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 671\n\nSo, subtract equation 3 from equation 9:\n\nd = 900 - 671 = 229\n\nSo, d = 229\n\nBut from equation 2: a + b + c + d = x => a + b + c + 229 = x\n\nBut from equation 2 and equation x + w = 229, since x + w = 229, we have:\n\na + b + c + 229 + w = 229 => a + b + c + w = 0\n\nWhich is impossible because a, b, c, w are non-negative.\n\nWait, this can't be. There must be a miscalculation.\n\nWait, equation 9: d + e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 900\n\nEquation 3: e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 671\n\nThus, equation 9 - equation 3: d = 900 - 671 = 229\n\nSo, d = 229\n\nFrom equation 2: x = a + b + c + d = a + b + c + 229\n\nFrom equation x + w = 229, we have:\n\n(a + b + c + 229) + w = 229\n\nTherefore:\n\na + b + c + w = 0\n\nWhich is impossible because a, b, c, w are non-negative. Thus, this suggests a contradiction.\n\nThis indicates that my model is wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the definitions.\n\nWait, let me think again.\n\nSince everyone owns D, the only way to own exactly one is to own only D.\n\nThus, x = d\n\nSimilarly, residents owning exactly two can be:\n\n- D and A\n\n- D and B\n\n- D and C\n\n- A, B, and D\n\nWait, no, exactly two would be two of the four, so D and one other, or A, B, C, and D. But since they own D, if they own exactly two, they must own D and one more.\n\nSimilarly, exactly three would be D and two others, or three including D.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"exactly three of these things\", so three out of four, including D.\n\nSimilarly, exactly four: all four.\n\nThus, the residents can be categorized as:\n\n- Exactly one: D only (d)\n\n- Exactly two: D and A, D and B, D and C, or D and something else, but not owning A, B, C exclusively.\n\nWait, actually, no. Exactly two would be owning two of the four, which can be:\n\n- D and A\n\n- D and B\n\n- D and C\n\n- A, B, and D (but that's three)\n\nWait, no. If someone owns D and A, that's two. If someone owns D, A, and B, that's three.\n\nThus, exactly two: those who own exactly D and one other (A, B, or C).\n\nSimilarly, exactly three: those who own D and two others.\n\nExactly four: all four.\n\nThus, the counts:\n\nLet\u2019s define:\n\n- d: exactly D only\n\n- e: exactly D and A\n\n- f: exactly D and B\n\n- g: exactly D and C\n\n- h: exactly D, A, and B\n\n- i: exactly D, A, and C\n\n- j: exactly D, B, and C\n\n- k: exactly D, A, B, and C\n\nThus, the total residents:\n\nd + e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 900\n\nGiven:\n\n- Exactly two: e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 437\n\n- Exactly three: h + i + j + k = 234\n\n- Exactly four: k = w\n\n- Exactly one: d = x\n\nFrom the total residents:\n\nd + e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 900\n\nBut from the given, e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 437, so:\n\nd + 437 = 900 => d = 463\n\nBut from x + w = 229, x = d = 463? That contradicts.\n\nWait, no. Wait, if d is exactly one: d = x = 229 - w.\n\nBut earlier, we have d = 463, which would mean x = 463, but x = 229 - w.\n\nThus, 463 = 229 - w => w = 229 - 463 = -234. Negative, which is impossible.\n\nThis suggests an inconsistency.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a wrong definition.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nTotal residents: d + e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 900\n\nGiven:\n\ne + f + g + h + i + j + k = 437 (exactly two)\n\nh + i + j + k = 234 (exactly three)\n\nThus, from the total residents:\n\nd + (e + f + g + h + i + j + k) = d + 437 = 900 => d = 463\n\nSimilarly, from h + i + j + k = 234, so the number of residents owning exactly three is 234, which includes those who own three of A, B, C, D.\n\nBut from the problem, exactly three is 234, so h + i + j + k = 234\n\nBut from the total, e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 437, so e + f + g = 437 - 234 = 203\n\nSimilarly, from the total:\n\nd + e + f + g + h + i + j + k = 900\n\nBut we also know:\n\nh + i + j + k = 234\n\nSo, d + e + f + g = 900 - 234 = 666\n\nBut e + f + g = 203, so d = 666 - 203 = 463\n\nThus, d = 463, which is the number of residents owning exactly D only.\n\nBut from the problem, x + w = 229, where x is exactly one (d) and w is all four (k). So, x = 463, which is inconsistent with x = 229 - w.\n\nThis is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the problem as stated is impossible, which can't be.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that the residents who own exactly two include those who own two of D, G, S, and candy hearts.\n\nSimilarly, exactly three includes those who own three of D, G, S, and candy hearts.\n\nBut I think I need to model it differently.\n\nLet me try another approach.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\n- Let a be the number of residents who own exactly one of D, G, S, and do not own candy hearts.\n\n- Let b be the number who own exactly two of D, G, S, and do not own candy hearts.\n\n- Let c be the number who own exactly three of D, G, S, and do not own candy hearts.\n\n- Let d be the number who own exactly four of D, G, S, and do not own candy hearts. (But this is impossible because they own candy hearts as well, so d=0)\n\n- Let e be the number who own exactly one of D, G, S, and also own candy hearts.\n\n- Let f be the number who own exactly two of D, G, S, and also own candy hearts.\n\n- Let g be the number who own exactly three of D, G, S, and also own candy hearts.\n\n- Let h be the number who own all four, D, G, S, and candy hearts.\n\nSo, total residents:\n\na + b + c + e + f + g + h = 900\n\nGiven:\n\n- Exactly two of D, G, S: f + e + b = 437\n\n- Exactly three of D, G, S: g + c + e + b = 234\n\n- All four: h = ?\n\n- Exactly one of D, G, S: a + e = x\n\n- Exactly one of D, G, S and candy hearts: e + f = ?\n\nWait, this is getting too complicated.\n\nPerhaps it's better to use the principle of inclusion-exclusion for four sets.\n\nGiven that everyone owns candy hearts, so the total is 900.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\n- |D| = 195\n\n- |G| = 367\n\n- |S| = 562\n\n- |CH| = 900\n\nWe need to find |D \u2229 G \u2229 S \u2229 CH| = h\n\nWe know:\n\n- Number owning exactly two: 437\n\n- Number owning exactly three: 234\n\n- Number owning exactly one: x = 229 - h\n\nFrom inclusion-exclusion:\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = |D| + |G| + |S| - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S|\n\nBut |D \u222a G \u222a S| = total residents - those who own nothing of D, G, S = 900 - (a + e) = 900 - (x + e). But x = 229 - h, so 900 - (229 - h + e) = 671 + h - e\n\nBut also, |D \u222a G \u222a S| = |D| + |G| + |S| - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S|\n\nWhich is 195 + 367 + 562 - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S|\n\nBut |D \u2229 G| includes those who own exactly two, three, or four of D, G, S, and CH.\n\nSimilarly, |D \u2229 G| = number of residents owning D and G, which is f + h + i (if i is D, G, S). Wait, this is too tangled.\n\nAlternatively, note that:\n\nThe number of residents owning exactly two of D, G, S is 437.\n\nThe number owning exactly three is 234.\n\nThe number owning all four is h.\n\nSo, the total number of residents owning at least one of D, G, S is 437 + 234 + h = 671 + h\n\nBut |D \u222a G \u222a S| must also equal 671 + h\n\nFrom inclusion-exclusion:\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = |D| + |G| + |S| - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S|\n\nBut |D \u222a G \u222a S| = 671 + h\n\nSo,\n\n195 + 367 + 562 - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S| = 671 + h\n\nCalculating 195 + 367 + 562: 195 + 367 = 562; 562 + 562 = 1124\n\nSo,\n\n1124 - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S| = 671 + h\n\nThus,\n\n(|D \u2229 G| + |D \u2229 S| + |G \u2229 S|) - |D \u2229 G \u2229 S| = 1124 - (671 + h) = 453 - h\n\nNow, |D \u2229 G|, |D \u2229 S|, |G \u2229 S| can be expressed in terms of the exact counts.\n\nEach intersection |D \u2229 G| includes:\n\n- Those owning exactly two (D and G): f\n\n- Those owning exactly three (D, G, S): i\n\n- Those owning all four: h\n\nSimilarly,\n\n|D \u2229 G| = f + i + h\n\n|D \u2229 S| = e + i + h\n\n|G \u2229 S| = f + i + h\n\n|D \u2229 G \u2229 S| = i + h\n\nTherefore,\n\n(|D \u2229 G| + |D \u2229 S| + |G \u2229 S|) - |D \u2229 G \u2229 S| = (f + i + h) + (e + i + h) + (f + i + h) - (i + h)\n\nSimplify:\n\n= f + i + h + e + i + h + f + i + h - i - h\n\n= 2f + e + 3i + h\n\nFrom earlier, this equals 453 - h\n\nSo,\n\n2f + e + 3i + h = 453 - h\n\nBut we need more equations.\n\nFrom the total residents:\n\na + b + c + d + e + f + g + h = 900