denote:\n\nLet\u2019s let h be the number of residents owning all four.\n\nThen, the number of residents owning exactly three is 234, which would be those owning three of D, G, S, and CH, but not all four.\n\nSimilarly, the number of residents owning exactly two is 437, which would be those owning two of D, G, S, and CH, but not all four.\n\nThe number of residents owning exactly one is x = 329 - h.\n\nNow, the total number of residents owning at least one of D, G, S is:\n\nx + y + z + h = (329 - h) + 437 + 234 + h = 329 - h + 437 + 234 + h = 329 + 437 + 234 = 1000\n\nWait, that can't be, because total residents are 900.\n\nSo, there must be an error.\n\nWait, actually, the total number of residents owning at least one of D, G, S is:\n\nx + y + z + h - 3*(those owning all four) because those owning all four are counted in each of D, G, S.\n\nWait, no.\n\nActually, using inclusion-exclusion, the total number of residents owning at least one of D, G, S is:\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = |D| + |G| + |S| - |D \u2229 G| - |D \u2229 S| - |G \u2229 S| + |D \u2229 G \u2229 S|\n\nWe know that:\n\n|D \u222a G \u222a S| = total residents - those owning nothing of D, G, S.\n\nBut everyone owns CH, but they might not own D, G, S.\n\nSo, |D \u222a G \u222a S| = 900 - (those owning only CH)\n\nBut we don't know those owning only CH.\n\nWait, actually, no. Those owning only CH are exactly x, the number of residents owning exactly one (which is D, G, S, and CH). Wait, no.\n\nWait, if x is the number of residents owning exactly one of D, G, S, and also owning CH.\n\nBut actually, the residents owning only CH are those who don't own D, G, S.\n\nSo, let\u2019s denote w = number of residents owning only CH.\n\nThus,\n\nw = total residents - |D \u222a G \u222a S|\n\nBut we don't know w.\n\nWait, but we can express |D \u222a G \u222a S| in terms of x, y, z, h.\n\nWait, no, because x, y, z, h are counts of residents owning D, G, S, and CH in different combinations.\n\nI think I'm stuck here.\n\nWait, let me try another approach.\n\nSince everyone owns CH, let's consider the number of residents owning D, G, S, and CH.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\n- a: own D only\n\n- b: own G only\n\n- c: own S only\n\n- d: own D and G only\n\n- e: own D and S only\n\n- f: own G and S only\n\n- g: own D, G, S only\n\n- h: own all four D, G, S, CH\n\nSo, total residents:\n\na + b + c + d + e + f + g + h = 900\n\nGiven:\n\nNumber of residents owning exactly two: d + e + f + g + h = 437 (Wait, no. Exactly two would be d, e, f, but not g or h)\n\nWait, actually, exactly two would be those owning exactly two of D, G, S, and CH.\n\nSo, exactly two: d, e, f, and also those owning two of D, G, S without CH? No, because everyone owns CH.\n\nWait, no, because everyone owns CH, so the only way to own exactly two is to own two of D, G, S, and also CH.\n\nThus, exactly two: d, e, f, and also those owning two of D, G, S and CH.\n\nBut actually, d, e, f are those owning exactly two of D, G, S, and CH.\n\nWait, no.\n\nWait, if d is D and G only, and also owns CH, then they own exactly three: D, G, CH.\n\nSimilarly, e is D and S only, and also owns CH: exactly three.\n\nf is G and S only, and also owns CH: exactly three.\n\nWait, this is confusing.\n\nWait, if someone owns exactly two of D, G, S, they must own two and also own CH, making it three items.\n\nBut the problem says residents owning exactly two of these things, which are D, G, S, and CH.\n\nWait, no. The problem says:\n\n\"Among the 900 residents of Aimeville, there are 195 who own a diamond ring, 367 who own a set of golf clubs, and 562 who own a garden spade. In addition, each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts. There are 437 residents who own exactly two of these things, and 234 residents who own exactly three of these things.\"\n\nSo, \"these things\" refers to diamond ring, golf clubs, garden spade, and bag of candy hearts.\n\nThus, residents can own any combination of these four, but each owns at least one.\n\nSo, exactly two: they own exactly two of these four.\n\nExactly three: they own exactly three of these four.\n\nExactly four: they own all four.\n\nExactly one: they own exactly one of these four.\n\nBut since everyone owns a bag of candy hearts, the residents can't own none of these four.\n\nThus, the breakdown is:\n\n- Exactly one: own only CH.\n\n- Exactly two: own two of D, G, S, CH.\n\n- Exactly three: own three of D, G, S, CH.\n\n- Exactly four: own all four.\n\nSo, let\u2019s define:\n\nLet a = exactly one (only CH)\n\nb = exactly two (two of D, G, S, CH)\n\nc = exactly three (three of D, G, S, CH)\n\nd = exactly four (all four)\n\nThus,\n\na + b + c + d = 900\n\nGiven:\n\nb = 437\n\nc = 234\n\nd = ?\n\na = 900 - (b + c + d) = 900 - (437 + 234 + d) = 900 - 671 - d = 229 - d\n\nWe need to find d.\n\nNow, we need to relate a, b, c, d using the counts of D, G, S.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nTotal D owners: 195 = number of residents owning D, which includes:\n\n- Those who own exactly D only: part of a\n\n- Those who own D and something else: b (exactly two) + c (exactly three) + d (exactly four)\n\nSimilarly,\n\nTotal G owners: 367 = those who own G only + those who own G and something else.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nTotal S owners: 562 = those who own S only + those who own S and something else.\n\nBut since everyone owns CH, the residents owning only D, G, S are part of a.\n\nLet\u2019s define:\n\nLet\u2019s break down the D owners:\n\n- Only D: a1\n\n- D and G: b1\n\n- D and S: b2\n\n- D, G, S: c1\n\n- All four: d\n\nSimilarly,\n\nTotal D owners: a1 + b1 + b2 + c1 + d = 195\n\nSimilarly for G:\n\n- Only G: a2\n\n- G and D: b1\n\n- G and S: b3\n\n- G, D, S: c1\n\n- All four: d\n\nTotal G owners: a2 + b1 + b3 + c1 + d = 367\n\nSimilarly for S:\n\n- Only S: a3\n\n- S and D: b2\n\n- S and G: b3\n\n- S, D, G: c1\n\n- All four: d\n\nTotal S owners: a3 + b2 + b3 + c1 + d = 562\n\nSimilarly, the residents owning exactly two:\n\nExactly two of D, G, S, CH:\n\n- D and G: b1\n\n- D and S: b2\n\n- G and S: b3\n\nBut each of these also owns CH, so they are part of exactly two.\n\nThus, total exactly two: b1 + b2 + b3 = 437\n\nSimilarly, exactly three:\n\n- D, G, S: c1\n\nThus, c1 = 234\n\nSimilarly, exactly four: d\n\nNow, from the D owners:\n\na1 + b1 + b2 + c1 + d = 195\n\nWe know c1 = 234, which would make:\n\na1 + b1 + b2 + 234 + d = 195 => a1 + b1 + b2 + d = -39\n\nWait, that can't be, since counts can't be negative.\n\nThis suggests a miscalculation.\n\nWait, c1 is the number of residents owning exactly three, which is D, G, S, and CH, so c1 = 234.\n\nThus, in the D owners, c1 is part of the D owners, so:\n\na1 + b1 + b2 + c1 + d = 195\n\nBut c1 = 234, so:\n\na1 + b1 + b2 + 234 + d = 195 => a1 + b1 + b2 + d = -39\n\nImpossible, as counts can't be negative.\n\nThis suggests that our assumption is wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps c1 is not 234, but c1 is the number of residents owning exactly three, which is 234.\n\nBut c1 is the number of residents owning exactly three, which is D, G, S, and CH.\n\nBut in the D owners, c1 is included in the count of D owners, so D owners include c1.\n\nSimilarly, G and S owners include c1.\n\nBut the problem is that if c1 = 234, then D owners would have a1 + b1 + b2 + 234 + d = 195, which is impossible.\n\nThus, perhaps our initial breakdown is wrong.\n\nAlternatively, maybe c1 is not 234, but rather the number of residents owning exactly three is 234, which includes those owning D, G, S, CH.\n\nBut in the counts of D, G, S, the c1 is part of the three-way intersection.\n\nBut let's think differently.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nLet\u2019s use inclusion-exclusion for four sets, but since everyone owns CH, it's a bit different.\n\nLet\u2019s define:\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\n- Let A = set of D owners\n\n- B = set of G owners\n\n- C = set of S owners\n\n- Let U = universe of 900 residents\n\nWe know:\n\n|A| = 195, |B| = 367, |C| = 562\n\nWe need to find |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = d\n\nWe also know:\n\nNumber of residents owni