four\n\n- |B \u2229 C| = G and S, includes exactly two (G, S), exactly three (D, G, S), and all four\n\nThus,\n\n|A \u2229 B| = |exactly two (D, G)| + |exactly three (D, G, S)| + |all four|\n\nSimilarly,\n\n|A \u2229 C| = |exactly two (D, S)| + |exactly three (D, G, S)| + |all four|\n\n|B \u2229 C| = |exactly two (G, S)| + |exactly three (D, G, S)| + |all four|\n\nBut we don't know the exact breakdown.\n\nBut we can express:\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\n- Let p = number of residents owning exactly two of D and G (i.e., D and G only)\n\n- Let q = number owning exactly two of D and S (D and S only)\n\n- Let r = number owning exactly two of G and S (G and S only)\n\n- Let s = number owning exactly three of D, G, S (D, G, S only)\n\n- Let t = number owning all four (D, G, S, CH)\n\nThus,\n\np + q + r + s + t = h\n\nBut wait, no. t is h, and s is part of exactly three.\n\nWait, actually:\n\n- Exactly two: p + q + r = 437\n\n- Exactly three: s = 234\n\n- All four: h\n\nBut h includes those in exactly four.\n\nWait, no. s is exactly three (D, G, S only), and h is exactly four (all four).\n\nThus,\n\nFrom exactly two: p + q + r = 437\n\nFrom exactly three: s = 234\n\nFrom all four: h = ?\n\nBut we need to relate |A \u2229 B|, |A \u2229 C|, |B \u2229 C|.\n\nCompute |A \u2229 B| = p + s + h\n\nSimilarly,\n\n|A \u2229 C| = q + s + h\n\n|B \u2229 C| = r + s + h\n\nThus,\n\n|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C| = (p + q + r) + 3s + 3h = (437) + 3*234 + 3h = 437 + 702 + 3h = 1139 + 3h\n\nAlso, from earlier:\n\n671 + h = 1124 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|\n\nBut |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| is the number of residents owning all four, which is h.\n\nWait, no. |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| includes those owning exactly three and all four.\n\nWait, actually, |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = s + h\n\nThus,\n\n671 + h = 1124 - (1139 + 3h) + (s + h)\n\nBut s = 234, so:\n\n671 + h = 1124 - 1139 - 3h + 234 + h\n\nSimplify:\n\n671 + h = (1124 - 1139 + 234) + (-3h + h)\n\n